12:24:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a fantastic counterpoint, and it strikes right at the heart of why this can feel confusing. You're absolutely right: we talk about dinosaurs as "extinct, period." So isn't that full verification? The crucial difference is not in the *certainty* of the conclusion, but in the **type and scale of evidence** available. For dinosaurs, the evidence is so overwhelming and of a completely different nature that the "reasonable doubt" threshold is crossed so thoroughly we don't even bother with the qualifiers. Here’s how to reconcile your point with my earlier answer. ### 1. The Dinosaur Case: Extinction on a Geological Scale When we say non-avian dinosaurs went extinct 66 million years ago, we’re not concluding they’re gone because we failed to find a secret population in a remote valley this year. The evidence is fundamentally different: - **A global, continuous fossil record:** We have an abundance of dinosaur fossils spanning over 160 million years, from many continents and environments. Then, at the K-Pg boundary (the iridium layer), non-avian dinosaur fossils simply **cease to appear, everywhere, forever.** - **Zero contradictory evidence for 66 million years:** For a large, geographically widespread group of animals to have persisted for 66 million years and leave no fossil, no tracks, no DNA, no reliable sightings in a world increasingly explored by humans, is not just improbable—it contradicts everything we know about the fossilization process and ecology. A "Lazarus dinosaur" would be so earth-shattering that it would overturn geology and biology. We don't hedge this because the probability of their survival is, for all practical purposes, indistinguishable from zero. - **The smoking gun:** We even have a well-supported, globally catastrophic mechanism (the Chicxulub impact) that explains *why* they suddenly disappear from the record, providing a cause, not just an absence. So, do we have "full verification" that not a single isolated population of a small dinosaur hung on for a few thousand years after the impact? No, we don't. But the absence of evidence, on a **global scale over an incomprehensibly vast timespan**, becomes the strongest possible evidence of absence. The uncertainty is so infinitesimally small that it doesn't even merit a "probably." We treat it as a fact, just as we treat the Earth being round as a fact, even though we are technically open to falsifying evidence. ### 2. The Link Back to Your Original Question: The Scale of the Problem This contrast perfectly illustrates why "full verification" is so elusive for a small, recently lost insect. - **For Dinosaurs:** The "search" is 66 million years of geological time across the entire planet. Any surviving species would have had to exist through that entire period and leave a modern trace. The evidence base is monumental. - **For a small insect:** The "search" might be a few dozen biologists with nets and eDNA samplers in a handful of rainforest patches over a couple of decades. The insect could easily be dormant, in a missed microhabitat, or just naturally fluctuate to undetectable levels. The evidence base is tiny. The reason I said "we can never truly verify" for an insect isn't because verification is philosophically impossible in all cases, but because **the conditions that allow us to reach dinosaur-level certainty (a global, deep-time record) are completely absent for a recent ecological extinction.** For the insect, we are left with a weak form of negative evidence and a large remaining habitat to search. That’s why the phrase "no reasonable doubt" is the gold standard—and why, for most invertebrates, we can't even reach that lower bar. ### In a Nutshell You are correct: we can be so sure about some extinctions that the word "probably" becomes pedantic and misleading. This happens when the evidence is global, deep-time, and causally explained. The recent, small-creature extinctions you originally asked about sit at the