e Passenger Pigeon, the search was plausibly exhaustive because they were huge, loud, and occurred in enormous flocks—so the “reasonable doubt” vanished much more quickly. ### So, Is Anything “Flat-Out Verified”? This gets to the heart of what “verification” means. In science, verification rarely means an absolute, logical proof of a universal negative. It means that the evidence is so overwhelming that the alternative (survival) would require a conspiracy of improbabilities that no sensible person entertains. For Martha’s case, the probability that a secret, viable population existed after her death and then also died out without leaving a single confirmed feather, photograph, or DNA trace for over a century is so infinitesimally small that we treat it as a flat fact. In the hands of a cautious epistemologist, however, it’s still a conclusion based on inference—just an incredibly strong one. ### Why Your Question Cuts to the Core of the Insect Problem The beauty of your question is that it highlights exactly why the extinction of a small insect can *never* reach this level of practical certainty. For a Martha-like event to happen with an insect, we would need: - Every single individual of that insect species to be in captivity, with a known count. - No wild population, not even a single overlooked egg or dormant larva in the soil. We simply never have that. For insects, we don’t know the original range well enough, we don’t know their population biology, and we can’t possibly check every square inch of habitat. So we are always stuck at the “inference from negative evidence” stage, never the “observed death of the last known individual” stage. Even when a lab colony of a critically endangered insect dies out, there might be an undiscovered wild population, or a dormant life stage waiting for the right rain. ### In Summary - **You’re right:** Martha’s death is as close to “flat-out verified” as conservation biology ever gets, because the species was conspicuous, hea