st patch of its habitat is paved over, you’ve caused an extinction. The precautionary principle is built into the system precisely because the evidence for so many species is so poor. Your line of questioning has traced the entire arc of this issue beautifully: - From "it's a settled fact" (for some), - to "it's not absolute, but the evidence is overwhelming" (for most declared extinctions), - to "for a tiny insect, we can't even gather the evidence to clear the reasonable doubt hurdle" (the core of my initial answer). Your final point is exactly right: extinction isn't philosophically special. But for small, overlooked creatures, the evidence is so thin that we genuinely cannot reach even the normal, practical level of scientific certainty we expect for a "fact." That leaves us with an uncomfortable, open-ended uncertainty that scientific language must reflect. That's the only reason it sounds so agnostic—not because other science is absolute, but because here, the evidence is uniquely impoverished. Thank you for pushing on this so relentlessly. It’s the kind of careful thinking that turns a simple question into a deep exploration of how we know anything at all.
